 b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/188860-general-politics-thread?p=191036#post191036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That doesn't stop the tone from being disrespectful. Nor the continued assumption that you understand things better than other people just because they aren't phrasing things too your liking.</div> </div> </div> </div> Considering you had to read my tone... yes, it does stop me from being disrespectful, but it doesn't stop you from constantly reading into my words what's not there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most? Or just most you've got experience with? </div> </div> </div> Most. It's a money thing. (Note this is a form of non-cogent induction.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I belong to a group the promotes planting and maintaining native wild fields near agricultural sites. It's entirely local. We're after both supporting local wildlife and decreasing the local impact of fertilizers, pesticides (and it turns out colony collapse disorder with bees). We're not after any sort of federal change. Because we don't need the government to do anything. Though grants are nice. </div> </div> </div> I'm glad you're involved, and going about it in what sounds like an effective manner. I fail to see where you fall into the category you believe I maligned...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most of us are also part of larger groups that you're more used to thinking of as being too naive. </div> </div> </div> I am?! Since when? Why didn't anyone tell me that I'm used to thinking of these larger organizations as being naive?!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But it isn't that we're naive. It's that there are local problems and their are larger problems. We need local activism and federal activism. </div> </div> </div> I never said you didn't, nor did I imply it. You, yourself, put that there. Not me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The bees are actually a good example of this. On one hand there's a huge amount of simple local things we can do about bees. And we really need them. But at the same time there is a national crisis here. If bee colonies continue to drop the impact on the agricultural industry is immense. We'll need to start paying people to pollinate crops, vastly increasing the price of food just to maintain current production levels.<br /> <br /> Local activism can help, a lot, but this is a problem that doesn't end at any line drawn on a map. The few counties that have managed to start repairing the damage done to bee populations can't magically replace all the colonies we've lost. They can't fix the problems that caused the collapse in the first place. That's why we also need better regulations on monocultures crops field distributions (monocultures are problematic, but the bigger problem is that we have giant fields of them that aren't broken up by other crops), and chemical applications over biological ones. </div> </div> </div> This is all good stuff to get involved with... but I fail to see how it goes against, &quot;most.&quot; All it says is that your circle might not fall under, &quot;Most.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That might be how politicians approach their issues of choice... but there are plenty of things that can wait. I'm pretty sure naming something else after someone can wait even if that gets done without much fuss.<br /> <br /> Tax code reform should get done... but it can wait. We'll collect taxes even if we're doing it in a suboptimal way.<br /> <br /> I think it is incredibly dangerous to just see it as, &quot;well in politics nothing can wait, so everything is equal,&quot; when in reality this simply isn't the case. Even if the country isn't going to get destroyed if infrastructure issues aren't addressed or climate change doesn't get acted on overnight, it doesn't mean we aren't digging ourselves so deep by not fixing it that we aren't heading to the point where &quot;fixing&quot; stops being the point and &quot;coping with the complete loss of something and trying to manage what to do&quot; is the new issue. </div> </div> </div> Yet in politics, nothing can wait. In reality, sure things can, but in politics? No they cannot. Because it makes a great talking point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is we are, going by the best science on the subject, on that point where if we don't start doing things now, not in 10 years, then we're going to get into cascade effects that we can't stop. We're already seeing the opening signs of those cascades getting set off. Politics has spent decades not doing enough when doing it would have been easier and we wouldn't be stuck in a situation of picking how bad do we want it.<br /> <br /> This sort of attitude is one of the reasons why climate change isn't moving. If you don't want to sound like denialist and just claim it doesn't matter, you can throw up your hands and say, &quot;well it's too late, so screw it.&quot; The longer we wait, the worse fixing it gets, until fixing it isn't an option any more and the slow death of larger nations starts setting in. </div> </div> </div> And what you want won't be fixed by US politics alone... and the other nations have proven recalcitrant. So... what are you asking for? (Though this could easily swing into a completely different topic on climate change politics.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even if not completely universally effective, there's plenty of prominent economic thinkers both outside of politics and inside of it that have solid proposals to do this. For example, it isn't going to fix everything, but eliminating the student debt bloating would certainly help stabilize the economy. I don't know any economic principle that says this problem is not a problem, or that fixing it will make it worse. Everything I've read on it says pretty universally that </div> </div> </div> Then perhaps you haven't read anything that disagrees with your position? (I'm not saying you are wrong.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The inability to prioritize issues by need and immediacy is a problem. And it doesn't have to be one. Waiting inherently means there are major issues that are going to get worse - possibly to the point of not being able to be fixed - so telling people that they have to suck it up and wait ~20 years before starting to fix it will even happen is basically just acknowledging that our system is going to drive itself off a cliff because we can't decided to apply brakes fast enough for anything that isn't war. </div> </div> </div> Not really. I recommend reading about the decline and fall of the Ottomans.<br /> <br /> A short version (massively under simplified), not related to the Ottomans, governments, our government especially, was not designed to move quickly. It was purposely designed to be slow, ponderous, and have to think through its decisions. Why? Because it's meant to be a long-lasting structure which has to concern itself with a hundred, or two hundred years from now. Some of the largest problems we get into are because we act rashly and don't consider the long term ramifications of our decisions. We reach compromises and great ideas that turn out to be very shallow or didn't go in the direction we thought they would.<br /> <br /> For instance, our founding fathers had some rather... foreshadowy quotes on the great compromise on slavery, that in 100 years time it would become an issue again. Almost 100 years to the day, the civil war broke out. Over an intemperate decision made to just move on and get the Articles of Confederation off the table instead of taking a stand. Modern day immigration is basically a massive cluster because we changed the Constitution to prevent former slaves from being disenfranchised (a good thing) and ended up enshrining an untenable system that we can't easily fix short of amending the Constitution.<br /> <br /> The Federal Reserve was created with an unlimited charter to replace lurching from one central bank to another every 10 years or so in the US. There were some rather... interesting results a bit over a decade later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T14:58:40-07:00","id":191715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/188860-general-politics-thread?p=191055#post191055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A more fundamental component, and something that I disagree with myself... the problem is that it is an advantage. So let's say the government of the US said &quot;nope, no more fractional reserve issues&quot; (which I consider slightly less likely than the sun exploding tomorrow), they can only enforce it on US banks or at best on US held assets. It's become an international standard, so besides the relative ease of shifting assets around on paper to make it work on paper, it results in a non-level playing field against the banks that *do* follow such a policy or law.</div> </div> </div> </div>I still remember the fun of an economics class in my masters program discussing how no debt is actually far, far worse for more corporations than carrying massive debt...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T15:01:17-07:00","id":192794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/188860-general-politics-thread?p=191713#post191713\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most. It's a money thing. (Note this is a form of non-cogent induction.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok. We don't seem to get on well. I'm largely considering not continuing with this but:<br /> <br /> Seriously. Please tell me this is a typo.<br /> <br /> Non-cogent induction is inherently to be considered false. The value of induction based reasoning is directly related to how cogent it is.<br /> <br /> Saying your reasoning is non-cogent and inductive means your premise(s) are either false, or there is no logically valid reasoning connecting the premise with the logic of the argument.<br /> <br /> So I'm left with three ways to read that and I'm baffled as to which you mean:<br /> <br /> 1) It's a typo and you didn't mean to say that at all, but I'm having trouble filling in what the possible mistake would have been.<br /> <br /> 2) You know how cogency and induction work, and are admitting that your reasoning is inherently nonsensical but you're insisting that your reasoning is valid anyway.<br /> <br /> 3) You are not familiar with how cogency and induction work and are simply using words incorrectly despite knowing what they mean in a non-specialist sense. However this draws some serious issues with how you've chosen to state other things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T16:19:23-07:00","id":192879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/188860-general-politics-thread?p=192794#post192794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ok. We don't seem to get on well. I'm largely considering not continuing with this but:<br /> <br /> Seriously. Please tell me this is a typo.<br /> <br /> Non-cogent induction is inherently to be considered false. The value of induction based reasoning is directly related to how cogent it is.<br /> <br /> Saying your reasoning is non-cogent and inductive means your premise(s) are either false, or there is no logically valid reasoning connecting the premise with the logic of the argument.<br /> <br /> So I'm left with three ways to read that and I'm baffled as to which you mean:<br /> <br /> 1) It's a typo and you didn't mean to say that at all, but I'm having trouble filling in what the possible mistake would have been.<br /> <br /> 2) You know how cogency and induction work, and are admitting that your reasoning is inherently nonsensical but you're insisting that your reasoning is valid anyway.<br /> <br /> 3) You are not familiar with how cogency and induction work and are simply using words incorrectly despite knowing what they mean in a non-specialist sense. However this draws some serious issues with how you've chosen to state other things.</div> </div> </div> </div>EDIT: Boy... really had to go back to my books on that one. No, I did mean non-cogent as in weak induction, as opposed to strong induction. Non cogent arguments are not inherently invalid, nor are they necessarily false in their premise, they can also be fundamentally untestable to a strong enough (k or n, whichever you prefer) case, sometimes referred to as, &quot;Not all relevant information can be examined.&quot;<br /> <br /> I have a premise here that fundamentally cannot be proven to be true, but nor is it necessarily false. That means the argument is not non-cogent, which is just a weak form of induction. It means it could still be true or false.<br /> <br /> Also, number 2 is not a sound conclusion in what you've listed above. It's valid in its own context, but not sound.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 07-10-2014, 01:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T16:31:25-07:00","id":192884,"title":"","content":"As opposed to if I had a premise which COULD be proven to be true, but I simply lack all the data... in which case, I could make a cogent statement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T16:36:57-07:00","id":192886,"title":"","content":"A question that could degenerate, but what the heck. <b>List your picks for your top five worst presidents</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T16:41:38-07:00","id":192887,"title":"","content":"1. Warren Harding.<br /> 2. Ulysses Grant... which I really feel bad for as he was a good general and by all accounts an honest man who surrounded himself with cronies and just... didn't... see it.<br /> 3. Millard Fillmore.<br /> 4. Franklin Pierce.<br /> 5. James Buchanan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T17:45:56-07:00","id":192906,"title":"","content":"So I was watching Senator Reid's press conference where he referred to the Hobby Lobby case as 5 white men deciding the fate of women. Later in the press conference he just says 5 men. I am wondering if, the 2nd time, he remembered that Justice Thomas is black.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-10T03:30:28-07:00","id":193363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/188860-general-politics-thread?p=192906#post192906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I was watching Senator Reid's press conference where he referred to the Hobby Lobby case as 5 white men deciding the fate of women. Later in the press conference he just says 5 men. I am wondering if, the 2nd time, he remembered that Justice Thomas is black.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what I've read about the decision, I'd say that there is no problem with the courts ruling. There is however a problem with <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religious_Freedom_Restoration_Act\" target=\"_blank\">RFRA</a>, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religious_Land_Use_and_Institutionalized_Persons_Act\" target=\"_blank\">RLUIPA</a> and the fact the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Department_of_Health_and_Human_Services\" target=\"_blank\">HHS</a> was already treating non-profits as persons under RFRA. An interesting historical note here is that it was Justice Scalia (Who was part of the majority ruling here) that back in 1993 wrote<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;To permit this would make the professed doctrines of religious belief superior to the law of the land, and in effect to permit every citizen to become a law unto himself. Generally applicable laws do not have to meet the standard of <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strict_scrutiny\" target=\"_blank\">strict scrutiny</a>, because such a requirement would create a private right to ignore generally applicable laws. </div> </div> </div>It was after that decision that congress passed RFRA for the express purpose of creating the private right to ignore generally applicable laws. Based on this and other comments by the court I don't feel characterising the judgement as made just because they're male Christians is particularly accurate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":292,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/292-zironic","user":"Zironic","date":". 07-10-2014, 06:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/292-zironic","name":"Zironic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar292_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"654"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-10T05:56:30-07:00","id":193439,"title":"","content":"<br /> <b>List your picks for your top five worst presidents</b><br /> <br /> <b>Franklin Pierce</b> for the Kansas-Nebraska Act. (Idiocy that expanded slavery)<br /> <b>Herbert Hoover </b>for &quot;Hoovervilles.&quot;<br /> <b>Andrew Jackson </b>for being a rotten shit (&quot;Trail of Tears&quot; and a supporter for slavery)<br /> <b>James Buchanan</b> because he was gay..(I'm kidding. LOL. Even though he was. He makes the list because he took a squat on Secession, which Lincoln had to clean up. Had he the courage, we could have had a rainbow flag proudly flying over the WH today. Alright, maybe that's a bit much, but still)<br /> <b>John Tyler</b> for being stupid enough to piss everyone off. And still no one knows who he is...<br /> <br /> Honorable mention goes to <b>Woodrow Wilson. </b>It's fair to say that a lot of presidents were pretty racist. But he was pretty extreme.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://